1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
They build battlements and bridges, roads and reservoirs.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
They are the largest public engineering concern in the

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
world. They have shaped and reshaped the

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
face of our continent. And they've helped boost us

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
into outer space. Now, the Army Corps of Engineers

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:45,769 --> 00:00:50,560
On July 26, 2001, in an act that seemed to defy the laws

8
00:00:50,561 --> 00:00:56,120
of nature, a massive 11,000-ton piece of concrete floated 27

9
00:00:56,121 --> 00:01:00,020
miles up the Ohio River to a final destination along the

10
00:01:00,021 --> 00:01:04,120
Monongahela River near Pittsburgh.

11
00:01:04,121 --> 00:01:10,260
At 333 feet long, 104 and 1 half feet wide, and 50 feet high,

12
00:01:10,261 --> 00:01:13,280
this portion of the new Braddock Dam, which is bigger than a

13
00:01:13,281 --> 00:01:17,480
football field, was the largest cast piece of concrete and rebar

14
00:01:17,481 --> 00:01:22,480
ever to set sail. This daring feat of engineering

15
00:01:22,481 --> 00:01:26,480
bravado was conceived, designed, and executed by the United

16
00:01:26,481 --> 00:01:28,480
States Army Corps of Engineers.

17
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:35,480
Built along the banks of the Ohio River, the giant slab of

18
00:01:35,481 --> 00:01:39,480
concrete marks the first time that a dam has been prefabricated

19
00:01:39,481 --> 00:01:43,480
in one place, then moved by water to another.

20
00:01:43,481 --> 00:01:48,480
But how to get a piece of concrete this massive to float?

21
00:01:48,481 --> 00:01:52,480
The answer lay in a simple yet elegant design feature.

22
00:01:52,481 --> 00:01:56,480
The dam's sections were designed with large empty baffles inside,

23
00:01:56,481 --> 00:01:59,480
which aided in flotation during the nautical journey.

24
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:07,480
Once accessorized with a gate and walls, the floating mass

25
00:02:07,481 --> 00:02:10,480
will be sunk onto a prepared underwater foundation near the

26
00:02:10,481 --> 00:02:15,480
Braddock River Lock. The empty chambers of the dam

27
00:02:15,481 --> 00:02:17,880
The empty chambers of the dam will be filled with more

28
00:02:17,881 --> 00:02:21,680
concrete, making it the first dam of its kind in history.

29
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:29,320
Tour de force engineering feats like this are the stock and

30
00:02:29,321 --> 00:02:33,820
trade of the Army Corps of Engineers, encompassing an

31
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:37,780
enormous range of projects and concerns, from the creation of a

32
00:02:37,781 --> 00:02:44,240
vast system of inland waterways and coastal harbors, to massive

33
00:02:44,241 --> 00:02:53,740
projects, to monumental architecture and design, to

34
00:02:53,741 --> 00:02:58,080
battlefield engineering, the Corps has responded for 200

35
00:02:58,081 --> 00:03:04,000
years in peace and war to the needs of our nation.

36
00:03:04,001 --> 00:03:07,200
Made up of soldiers and civilians, scientists, and

37
00:03:07,201 --> 00:03:11,200
specialists, the Corps accomplishes these projects

38
00:03:11,201 --> 00:03:14,800
network of eight divisions, broken up into 41 district

39
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:17,300
offices in the US and throughout the world.

40
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:23,240
The mission has evolved over time, from constructing

41
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:27,180
fortifications along the coastlines in the early 19th

42
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:31,360
century to today, where we do environmental restoration in the

43
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:34,460
Everglades. And we've done all missions in

44
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:38,920
between, from constructing air bases around the world to

45
00:03:38,921 --> 00:03:44,920
to support global reach, to the space program where we were

46
00:03:44,921 --> 00:03:48,920
instrumental in building the facilities at Cape Canaveral,

47
00:03:48,921 --> 00:03:53,920
to wrapping up for World Wars I and II where we did vast

48
00:03:53,921 --> 00:03:56,920
military construction for all the armed services around the

49
00:03:56,921 --> 00:04:00,920
world. Once strictly military in its

50
00:04:00,921 --> 00:04:03,920
makeup and assignments, the Corps today utilizes a force of

51
00:04:03,921 --> 00:04:07,920
more than 35,000 people, including by the end of the

52
00:04:07,921 --> 00:04:13,420
including biologists, engineers, geologists, hydrologists,

53
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:19,560
cartographers, natural resource managers, and combat soldiers.

54
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:22,160
As the world's largest engineering agency,

55
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:27,680
the Corps' philosophy and motto imply that nothing is impossible.

56
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:30,880
Essay on means let us try.

57
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:33,780
It's a French term.

58
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:40,580
We adopted it because we think it's the spirit of our Corps.

59
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:44,920
Because let us try is not a request, it's a statement.

60
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:47,920
Let us try.

61
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:49,820
General George Washington recognized

62
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:52,340
the need for skilled engineers in the earliest days

63
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:55,240
of the Revolutionary War.

64
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:57,840
One of the first tasks for colonial engineers

65
00:04:57,940 --> 00:04:59,780
was the creation of fortifications

66
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:06,780
at Bunker Hill in 1775. The earliest corps of engineers

67
00:05:06,781 --> 00:05:11,720
were known as sappers and miners, from the French word sapeurs.

68
00:05:11,721 --> 00:05:15,620
The sap comes from the term of constructing a trench to attack

69
00:05:15,621 --> 00:05:19,240
a fortification, and so they called the people that did this

70
00:05:19,241 --> 00:05:22,740
sappers. Miners come from burrowing under

71
00:05:22,741 --> 00:05:27,240
the ground to blow up or to cave in whatever that fortification

72
00:05:27,241 --> 00:05:31,240
fortification wall was. The Corps saw its finest hour

73
00:05:31,241 --> 00:05:36,740
at the siege of Yorktown in 1781. Its mission, cut a path through

74
00:05:36,741 --> 00:05:40,840
British General Earl Cornwallis' fortifications so that American

75
00:05:40,841 --> 00:05:45,000
and French infantry could break through and wage battle with 8,000

76
00:05:45,001 --> 00:05:51,000
British troops. What lay between American troops

77
00:05:51,001 --> 00:05:54,680
and enemy forces was a series of field fortifications known as

78
00:05:54,681 --> 00:06:01,520
redoubts. A redoubt is a strong point in your fortifications. It's reinforced by

79
00:06:01,521 --> 00:06:07,657
artillery, it has infantry support, and it also has impediments to attacking forces.

80
00:06:07,658 --> 00:06:08,680
There would be

81
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:16,840
constructions of trees that were chopped down and sharpened and stuck into the earth defenses.

82
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:21,960
This type of defense had to be broken through by the engineers, so they would go forward under fire

83
00:06:21,961 --> 00:06:24,360
to clear a path through this defense,

84
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:27,700
and then the infantry would attack through that path.

85
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:29,760
The victory at Yorktown dealt the British

86
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:34,700
a final decisive blow that brought an end to the war.

87
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:37,300
In 1802, Congress recognized the need

88
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:40,220
for a permanent corps of engineers.

89
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:44,420
That same year, the US Academy at West Point was established.

90
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:47,320
West Point was the first engineering school founded

91
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:51,960
in America, and the law that formed West Point

92
00:06:51,961 --> 00:06:56,860
also formed the organization we know today as the US Army Corps

93
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:58,200
of Engineers.

94
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:01,540
It headquartered it at West Point and put the Army Corps

95
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:05,700
of Engineers in charge of the military academy.

96
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,440
Throughout the 19th century, the defense of America

97
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:11,920
was the Corps' main task, and it was responsible for the

98
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:17,060
construction of a vast system of coastal harbors and fortifications.

99
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:20,960
But a new mandate was emerging, civil projects.

100
00:07:20,961 --> 00:07:23,700
The Corps of Engineers was given the Civil Works mission

101
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:25,760
not simply because of its proficiency

102
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:29,200
as an engineering organization, but because the country,

103
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:31,600
meaning the executive branch in Congress,

104
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:34,480
thought that civil works and national defense

105
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:36,300
were closely connected.

106
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:38,040
You needed good river routes.

107
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:40,680
You needed good roads, not only for commerce,

108
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:43,080
but to move armies around.

109
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:45,480
In 1824, the Corps was charged

110
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:48,460
with improving navigation on the Ohio and Mississippi

111
00:07:48,461 --> 00:07:52,460
rivers, where ancient log rafts, massive tree limbs,

112
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:55,460
and unseen sandbars posed deadly obstacles

113
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:58,900
for early riverboats.

114
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,300
The Corps of Engineers sponsored a contest.

115
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:09,280
How would they remove this forest from the river?

116
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:13,020
All kinds of contraptions were suggested and invented.

117
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:16,260
There were great river kites that would jerk out a snag

118
00:08:16,261 --> 00:08:18,760
snag with the force of the current.

119
00:08:18,761 --> 00:08:20,860
There were grappling hooks.

120
00:08:20,861 --> 00:08:24,260
There were diving bells.

121
00:08:24,261 --> 00:08:27,160
One innovation that worked the best was the steam-powered

122
00:08:27,161 --> 00:08:31,160
snag boat, patented in 1838 by steamboat captain and core

123
00:08:31,161 --> 00:08:35,660
employee Henry Miller Shreve.

124
00:08:35,661 --> 00:08:39,060
Designed to break up even the most tangled obstacles, Shreve's

125
00:08:39,061 --> 00:08:43,660
Heliopolis consisted of two boats with iron-plated bows and

126
00:08:43,661 --> 00:08:47,960
and an iron-plated cross piece, the snag beam.

127
00:08:47,961 --> 00:08:51,560
And what would happen is the operator of the vessel would

128
00:08:51,561 --> 00:08:54,900
drive that vessel full power into a snag.

129
00:08:54,901 --> 00:08:58,200
The snag would get caught on that beam, and it would be ripped

130
00:08:58,201 --> 00:09:00,520
up from the river bottom, and then it could be dragged on

131
00:09:00,521 --> 00:09:03,020
the vessel where it could be sawed up into chunks or towed

132
00:09:03,021 --> 00:09:06,120
to the shoreline. Shreve and his fleet of

133
00:09:06,121 --> 00:09:08,660
snag boats cleared tens of thousands of snags from the

134
00:09:08,661 --> 00:09:12,660
Ohio and Mississippi rivers. The town of Shreveport,

135
00:09:12,661 --> 00:09:16,560
Louisiana was named in his honor.

136
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:20,000
Snag boats were fine for log jams and other debris, but what

137
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:23,900
if the hazard was mud? Sandbars and shallow sections

138
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,300
of the river could prove just as deadly to navigation.

139
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:31,620
One way to deepen rivers and harbors was to remove sediment

140
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:35,920
by dredging. The Corps of Engineers

141
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:39,420
devised a fleet of dredge boats that scooped out the soft mud.

142
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:45,400
Sometimes the Mississippi itself was used to improve

143
00:09:45,401 --> 00:09:46,900
navigation.

144
00:09:46,901 --> 00:09:50,400
Dykes made of piled boulders and dredge material were used to

145
00:09:50,401 --> 00:09:53,800
decrease the width of the river, thereby increasing the velocity

146
00:09:53,801 --> 00:09:57,400
of water passing by. The scouring action of the fast

147
00:09:57,401 --> 00:10:03,020
water automatically carved a deeper channel in the river.

148
00:10:03,021 --> 00:10:06,360
As navigation improved, the rivers carried settlers and

149
00:10:06,361 --> 00:10:09,720
commerce farther and farther west.

150
00:10:09,721 --> 00:10:13,020
The Corps was given the task of mapping the way, as well as

151
00:10:13,021 --> 00:10:15,820
creating some of the most important roads into the

152
00:10:15,821 --> 00:10:20,160
uncharted wilderness. By the end of the century, the

153
00:10:20,161 --> 00:10:23,760
Corps had evolved from a small band of trench diggers into an

154
00:10:23,761 --> 00:10:27,480
experienced engineering force whose on-the-job training made

155
00:10:27,481 --> 00:10:31,380
westward expansion possible. But the nation's rugged

156
00:10:31,381 --> 00:10:35,720
landscape would continue to pose enormous challenges.

157
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:43,120
The Mississippi, as Mark Twain said in the 1880s, was the

158
00:10:43,121 --> 00:10:47,520
backbone of the nation. It was America's highway of

159
00:10:47,521 --> 00:10:51,120
industrialization, but it was also the drainage point for

160
00:10:51,121 --> 00:10:55,120
over 40% of the country's watershed, erupting at times into

161
00:10:55,121 --> 00:11:02,120
flooding of epic proportions. The Army Corps of Engineers was

162
00:11:02,121 --> 00:11:06,120
called in to tackle the problem in 1879 with the creation of the

163
00:11:06,121 --> 00:11:09,720
of the Mississippi River Commission.

164
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:12,460
Corps and civilian members of the commission were charged with

165
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:16,160
devising a sound flood control plan, as well as improving

166
00:11:16,260 --> 00:11:20,660
navigation. At this time, levees were the

167
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:25,980
principal form of flood control used to harness the Mississippi.

168
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:29,980
A levee is an earthen embankment that is used to

169
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:34,780
provide flood protection to the lands adjacent to a river.

170
00:11:34,781 --> 00:11:39,820
It confines the flows of the river to a certain path that's

171
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,480
designated along the river system.

172
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:48,720
On the Mississippi River, we have some 22 million acres

173
00:11:48,820 --> 00:11:53,840
of alluvial lands, lands that have once been overflowed

174
00:11:53,940 --> 00:11:56,740
by the river.

175
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:00,080
Beginning in the 1890s, thousands of miles of levees

176
00:12:00,180 --> 00:12:03,480
were constructed along the Mississippi, some as high as 40

177
00:12:03,481 --> 00:12:07,720
feet. Monumental flooding in 1912 and

178
00:12:07,721 --> 00:12:11,980
1913 raised doubts about whether levees alone were enough protection

179
00:12:11,981 --> 00:12:18,960
from the mighty Mississippi. Then, in 1927, a catastrophic

180
00:12:18,961 --> 00:12:22,840
flood hit the region. The 27th flood, there was,

181
00:12:22,841 --> 00:12:28,740
first off, just tremendous rainfalls, and the flood stages

182
00:12:28,741 --> 00:12:32,780
reached levels that exceeded the levee systems.

183
00:12:32,781 --> 00:12:36,280
It overtaxed the levee systems with many failing from under

184
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:40,460
seepage and many failing from overtopping.

185
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:45,200
There was torrents of water that raged through the delta areas.

186
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:48,960
People would wake up at night with water in their homes

187
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:54,440
and have to crawl to the top of the rooftops to reach safety.

188
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:58,680
Eventually, they ended up along pieces of the levee system

189
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:02,180
that had not failed.

190
00:13:02,181 --> 00:13:08,160
500 people were killed and 500,000 left homeless.

191
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:10,360
Millions of acres were flooded for months

192
00:13:10,460 --> 00:13:12,760
after the river had crested.

193
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:16,420
The 1928 Flood Control Act gave the Corps

194
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:19,560
the responsibility for developing a plan of flood

195
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:23,200
protection for the valley.

196
00:13:23,300 --> 00:13:26,040
Chief of Engineers, Major General Edgar Jadwin,

197
00:13:26,140 --> 00:13:30,300
planned the construction of enormous concrete floodways,

198
00:13:30,301 --> 00:13:33,700
channels consisting of massive walls that redirect the flow of

199
00:13:33,701 --> 00:13:39,200
water away from the swollen river, and floodgates that

200
00:13:39,201 --> 00:13:42,340
allow the water to flow to another area, such as Lake

201
00:13:42,341 --> 00:13:49,260
Pontchartrain in New Orleans. In the late 1930s,

202
00:13:49,261 --> 00:13:52,460
reservoirs were incorporated into the plan.

203
00:13:52,461 --> 00:13:55,660
Constructed on major tributaries of the Mississippi, the

204
00:13:55,661 --> 00:13:59,060
reservoirs would collect flood water, slowly regulating its

205
00:13:59,061 --> 00:14:01,700
release into the main river until the flood eventually

206
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:02,700
subsided.

207
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,400
A secondary but serious problem with flooding

208
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:11,400
is that a fast-moving river can cause erosion of its banks.

209
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,120
So the Corps perfected the design of revetments,

210
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:20,820
a major weapon in the war against erosion.

211
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:23,520
In the early stages of the Corps'

212
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:26,820
involvement of flood protection, they

213
00:14:26,821 --> 00:14:32,860
They used wooden mattresses of willows

214
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:34,860
to serve as bank protection.

215
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:39,340
And this was just a mass of willow trees that

216
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:42,540
were woven together, braided together,

217
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:45,200
and laid along the bank.

218
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:47,800
Revetment evolved into sophisticated construction

219
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:52,040
of articulated concrete block sections bound with steel cable

220
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:53,380
to form a flexible mattress.

221
00:14:53,381 --> 00:14:59,380
Vast sections were unrolled from a barge blanketing the bottom

222
00:14:59,381 --> 00:15:01,760
of the river, as well as the banks,

223
00:15:01,761 --> 00:15:04,760
to form a new permanent river siding.

224
00:15:04,761 --> 00:15:07,060
Some sections of the famously winding river

225
00:15:07,061 --> 00:15:10,200
were too difficult to try and save from erosion.

226
00:15:10,201 --> 00:15:12,660
Engineers realized that by straightening the river

227
00:15:12,661 --> 00:15:16,340
with cutoffs, they could also shorten it.

228
00:15:16,341 --> 00:15:17,800
When we straightened the river,

229
00:15:17,801 --> 00:15:21,500
we would just cut the neck of a bend in the river off.

230
00:15:21,501 --> 00:15:22,201
off.

231
00:15:22,202 --> 00:15:28,920
And what that did, it resulted in a shorter river, but a more efficient channel on the river.

232
00:15:28,921 --> 00:15:35,580
And so it provided flood protection by carrying more flow at a lower stage in the river system.

233
00:15:36,340 --> 00:15:39,200
While most of these river modifications proved beneficial,

234
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:42,559
some had unforeseen consequences.

235
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:47,140
A cutoff made by none other than Captain Henry Shreve in 1831

236
00:15:47,141 --> 00:15:50,740
had set off a chain reaction that engineers today are still

237
00:15:50,741 --> 00:15:52,140
working to contain.

238
00:15:52,141 --> 00:15:54,640
He went in and made the river shorter

239
00:15:54,641 --> 00:15:57,140
in a specific location.

240
00:15:57,141 --> 00:16:00,440
But when he did that, the river reacted to it,

241
00:16:00,441 --> 00:16:04,940
and Mother Nature always wins out.

242
00:16:04,941 --> 00:16:07,440
Because of Shreve's cutoff, by 1950,

243
00:16:07,441 --> 00:16:10,340
over 30% of the Red River and the Mississippi

244
00:16:10,341 --> 00:16:12,840
were flowing into the Atchafalaya.

245
00:16:12,841 --> 00:16:15,840
Engineers realized that unless something was done,

246
00:16:15,841 --> 00:16:17,840
The entire flow of the Mississippi would eventually

247
00:16:17,841 --> 00:16:22,740
flood the Atchafalaya. Cities in southeastern Louisiana,

248
00:16:22,741 --> 00:16:25,980
including New Orleans, would be left high and dry, while

249
00:16:25,981 --> 00:16:28,780
southwestern Louisiana would be inundated.

250
00:16:28,781 --> 00:16:32,080
To find a solution, design engineers at the Waterways

251
00:16:32,081 --> 00:16:35,800
Experiment Station in Vicksburg, Mississippi, built a model of

252
00:16:35,801 --> 00:16:38,600
the river. They created two structures

253
00:16:38,601 --> 00:16:42,000
to control the flow of water. This is the low-sill

254
00:16:42,001 --> 00:16:47,900
structure, and it was operational 365 days a year.

255
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,740
The low-sill structure strictly regulates the flow of water

256
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:54,140
from the Mississippi into the Atchafalaya, keeping the Old

257
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:59,260
River on its present course. The overbank structure only

258
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:02,960
operates during floods, allowing a controlled amount of water to

259
00:17:03,060 --> 00:17:07,160
be gradually discharged across the floodplain.

260
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:10,660
For a while, it seemed as if the Corps had managed to tame the

261
00:17:10,661 --> 00:17:16,340
tamed the Mississippi. But severe flooding in 1973 once

262
00:17:16,341 --> 00:17:19,140
again threatened the lower region, and the low-cell

263
00:17:19,141 --> 00:17:22,340
structure was severely damaged.

264
00:17:22,341 --> 00:17:26,100
At that time, there was a mirror image of a wing wall

265
00:17:26,101 --> 00:17:28,980
similar to what's on the other side of the structure. That

266
00:17:28,981 --> 00:17:32,340
wing wall failed, a significant portion of the structure was

267
00:17:32,341 --> 00:17:35,620
undermined, means the Corps of Engineers couldn't shut down the

268
00:17:35,621 --> 00:17:39,220
structure because we had to maintain our duty as far as

269
00:17:39,221 --> 00:17:42,820
of water leaving the Mississippi River.

270
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:45,320
In the years following the 1973 flood,

271
00:17:45,420 --> 00:17:48,960
the Corps of Engineers not only improved the low-sill structure

272
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:51,560
but conceived of a stopgap measure to further protect

273
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:55,460
the region from flooding, an auxiliary structure.

274
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:57,380
The purpose of the auxiliary structure

275
00:17:57,480 --> 00:18:00,780
was really twofold. Reduce the amount of flow that

276
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:05,320
had to be passed through the low-sill structure.

277
00:18:05,420 --> 00:18:08,280
And we also designed it to help maybe control the amount of

278
00:18:08,281 --> 00:18:11,580
amount of sediment that was being diverted from the Mississippi

279
00:18:11,581 --> 00:18:15,080
River into the Atchafalaya River also.

280
00:18:15,081 --> 00:18:17,080
The Corps' experience on the Mississippi

281
00:18:17,081 --> 00:18:20,420
has shown that the technology of river control has to be as

282
00:18:20,421 --> 00:18:25,040
dynamic as the river itself. Major floods do continue to

283
00:18:25,041 --> 00:18:29,040
occur along the Mississippi, but as technology has evolved,

284
00:18:29,041 --> 00:18:32,240
damage and loss of life along this great river have been

285
00:18:32,241 --> 00:18:36,980
greatly reduced. One tool of flood control,

286
00:18:36,981 --> 00:18:40,480
reservoir evolved into yet another area of expertise for

287
00:18:40,481 --> 00:18:45,620
the Army Corps of Engineers. Throughout the 20th century,

288
00:18:45,621 --> 00:18:48,820
the Corps has constructed dozens of reservoirs and hydropower

289
00:18:48,821 --> 00:18:53,440
dams on many of America's major rivers.

290
00:18:53,441 --> 00:18:56,840
One of the most spectacular of its time was the Bonneville

291
00:18:56,841 --> 00:19:00,440
Dam, part of a network of 12 Corps dams on the Columbia and

292
00:19:00,441 --> 00:19:05,440
Snake Rivers in the Pacific Northwest.

293
00:19:05,441 --> 00:19:10,240
Built between 1933 and 1937, Bonneville was one of the

294
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:13,240
largest hydroelectric projects of the New Deal.

295
00:19:13,340 --> 00:19:16,980
5,000 people were employed around the clock, building the

296
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:23,000
2,690-foot-long and 197-foot-high dam and two-generator

297
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:25,200
powerhouse.

298
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:28,800
Bonneville also brought to the region a navigational wonder,

299
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:32,100
the largest single-lift lock in the world at the time of

300
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:34,500
construction.

301
00:19:34,501 --> 00:19:37,300
A lock sections a portion of the river channel,

302
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:39,440
and by flooding this chamber with water,

303
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:41,840
a vessel in the lock can be raised or lowered

304
00:19:41,940 --> 00:19:46,240
past a difficult section of the river.

305
00:19:46,340 --> 00:19:49,140
But perhaps the most impressive achievement at Bonneville

306
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:52,720
was its innovative series of fish ladders incorporated

307
00:19:52,820 --> 00:19:55,660
into the very design of the dam.

308
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:58,200
In their quest to get upstream to spawn,

309
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:02,200
several species of salmon search out and find the fish ladders,

310
00:20:02,201 --> 00:20:05,700
Ladders, a series of graduated steps and pools that provide a

311
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:07,300
protected passage around the dam.

312
00:20:10,600 --> 00:20:13,140
This is the adult fish ladder for the Bonneville

313
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:14,540
Second Tower House.

314
00:20:14,640 --> 00:20:17,340
It is one of four adult fish ladders at the Bonneville

315
00:20:17,440 --> 00:20:21,360
Project for adult fish that are migrating upriver to spawn can

316
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:23,960
get up and around Bonneville Dam.

317
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:27,700
It is one of the newer designs of fish ladders in the Columbia

318
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:31,460
River system, and it is a very effective fish passage system.

319
00:20:31,461 --> 00:20:35,300
probably about 16,000 adult salmon and steelhead that

320
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:40,260
moved up this ladder today. The construction of dams,

321
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:42,900
locks, and other civil projects had helped the Corps become

322
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,000
America's premier engineering agency.

323
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:49,320
But few realize the influence the Corps has had on the design

324
00:20:49,420 --> 00:20:51,820
and construction of our nation's capital.

325
00:20:55,960 --> 00:20:58,720
The Army Corps of Engineers has played a conspicuous role

326
00:20:58,721 --> 00:21:04,620
wars, emergencies, and the construction of the nation's

327
00:21:04,621 --> 00:21:09,260
infrastructure. But not many people know that

328
00:21:09,261 --> 00:21:12,960
it is largely responsible for the design, construction, and

329
00:21:12,961 --> 00:21:18,480
look of Washington, D.C. In 1791, former army engineer

330
00:21:18,481 --> 00:21:22,220
Pierre-Charles L'Enfant designed the master plan for the new

331
00:21:22,221 --> 00:21:24,780
capital. The plan that L'Enfant

332
00:21:24,781 --> 00:21:28,780
came up with is very reminiscent of Paris.

333
00:21:28,781 --> 00:21:31,380
He learned, well, he was a Frenchman, for one thing, but

334
00:21:31,381 --> 00:21:35,380
with its vistas and grand avenues planned to be a certain width

335
00:21:35,381 --> 00:21:38,380
with trees lining, with monuments at appropriate

336
00:21:38,381 --> 00:21:42,880
locations that could be seen from a great distance.

337
00:21:42,881 --> 00:21:45,880
Army Corps engineer First Lieutenant Montgomery C.

338
00:21:45,881 --> 00:21:48,880
Meigs also left a lasting impression on the District of

339
00:21:48,881 --> 00:21:53,380
Columbia. In 1853, he was given the

340
00:21:53,381 --> 00:21:56,880
assignment to supply fresh water to the district.

341
00:21:56,980 --> 00:21:59,020
Meggs was a man of vision.

342
00:21:59,120 --> 00:22:02,120
He wasn't just trying to solve the immediate problem

343
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:05,120
of a supply of water for a capital city.

344
00:22:05,220 --> 00:22:09,560
He was looking to the fact that this city was going to be

345
00:22:09,660 --> 00:22:13,860
probably the capital of a nation that was going to continue to

346
00:22:13,960 --> 00:22:17,040
grow, and its water needs were going to be far beyond what

347
00:22:17,140 --> 00:22:21,000
anyone could even imagine in the mid-19th century.

348
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:22,840
One part of Meggs' water planning

349
00:22:22,841 --> 00:22:27,840
included the construction of two bridges, one over the Cabin

350
00:22:27,841 --> 00:22:32,340
John Creek and the other over Rock Creek.

351
00:22:32,341 --> 00:22:34,980
The bridges not only carried traffic into the city,

352
00:22:34,981 --> 00:22:37,840
they carried fresh water in pipes designed as an integral

353
00:22:37,841 --> 00:22:38,840
part of the bridge.

354
00:22:42,140 --> 00:22:44,840
Meigs also supervised the expansion of the Capitol

355
00:22:44,841 --> 00:22:45,840
building.

356
00:22:45,841 --> 00:22:48,640
When two new wings were added to the Capitol,

357
00:22:48,641 --> 00:22:51,640
the old dome began to look too small in relationship to the

358
00:22:51,641 --> 00:22:55,640
of the building. A new dome was designed that

359
00:22:55,740 --> 00:22:57,640
consisted of cast and wrought iron.

360
00:23:01,640 --> 00:23:04,680
When it was completed, this impressive addition to the capital

361
00:23:04,780 --> 00:23:09,600
weighed almost 9 million pounds.

362
00:23:09,700 --> 00:23:11,700
Throughout this time of capital improvements,

363
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:15,300
the Corps still had to remain true to its original mandate,

364
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:19,300
defensive structures. For the entire Civil War,

365
00:23:19,301 --> 00:23:21,300
the city had to be fortified,

366
00:23:21,301 --> 00:23:23,300
and the fortification of Washington

367
00:23:23,301 --> 00:23:26,300
fell to the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.

368
00:23:26,301 --> 00:23:29,300
And so if you would look at the maps of Washington

369
00:23:29,301 --> 00:23:32,300
during the Civil War, you would see a series of forts

370
00:23:32,301 --> 00:23:34,300
that ringed the Capitol.

371
00:23:34,301 --> 00:23:36,300
And these forts were constructed

372
00:23:36,301 --> 00:23:38,300
by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.

373
00:23:38,301 --> 00:23:43,300
In those days, if you had a corps castle on your cap shield,

374
00:23:43,301 --> 00:23:45,300
you did not buy a drink and you didn't buy food

375
00:23:45,301 --> 00:23:47,300
in any establishment.

376
00:23:47,301 --> 00:23:50,900
They were provided for engineers in thanks for the work that they

377
00:23:50,901 --> 00:23:53,900
were doing in protecting the city.

378
00:23:53,901 --> 00:23:57,040
It wasn't until the end of the 19th century that the Corps

379
00:23:57,041 --> 00:24:00,740
made some of its most lasting imprints on Washington, D.C.,

380
00:24:00,741 --> 00:24:04,060
with the unlikely marriage of land reclamation and great

381
00:24:04,061 --> 00:24:08,560
monuments. The land along the Potomac River

382
00:24:08,561 --> 00:24:12,000
in Washington, D.C. was subject to periodic flooding that at

383
00:24:12,001 --> 00:24:15,500
times reached all the way to the White House.

384
00:24:15,501 --> 00:24:18,500
First, the Corps dredged a channel that led to a tidal

385
00:24:18,501 --> 00:24:23,500
basin, built to contain and control the tidal flows.

386
00:24:23,501 --> 00:24:26,500
The Corps then used the dredge material to build up the land

387
00:24:26,501 --> 00:24:30,500
previously flooded by the tides. This reclaimed land became

388
00:24:30,501 --> 00:24:34,500
Potomac Park, the waterfront parkland where monuments such

389
00:24:34,501 --> 00:24:38,500
as the Washington, Lincoln, and Jefferson Memorials now stand.

390
00:24:38,501 --> 00:24:41,500
When we think about the monumental structures in

391
00:24:41,501 --> 00:24:45,600
Washington, D.C. The Washington Monument and the Lincoln

392
00:24:45,601 --> 00:24:49,300
Memorial were monuments that the Corps of Engineers had a direct

393
00:24:49,301 --> 00:24:53,340
role in completing. The Corps took over the

394
00:24:53,341 --> 00:24:57,340
completion of the Washington Monument in 1876, after a

395
00:24:57,341 --> 00:24:59,980
private organization could no longer cope with construction

396
00:24:59,981 --> 00:25:02,620
costs, as well as the engineering challenge the

397
00:25:02,621 --> 00:25:07,020
structure posed. At the time it was built,

398
00:25:07,021 --> 00:25:10,420
the Washington Monument was the tallest structure of its type in

399
00:25:10,421 --> 00:25:14,660
world, and it presented a real challenge to anchor that in the

400
00:25:14,661 --> 00:25:18,820
kind of land that we had here in Washington, D.C., land that had

401
00:25:18,821 --> 00:25:21,460
been reclaimed, that was formerly swamped.

402
00:25:21,461 --> 00:25:24,500
This was a real challenge, and it was a challenge that the

403
00:25:24,501 --> 00:25:29,380
Corps of Engineers officers met quite well.

404
00:25:29,381 --> 00:25:32,500
Corps engineers redesigned the foundation of the Washington

405
00:25:32,501 --> 00:25:35,780
Monument, strengthening it so it could support a structure that

406
00:25:35,781 --> 00:25:40,420
would ultimately weigh more than 40,000 tons.

407
00:25:40,421 --> 00:25:44,180
The walls of the sleek obelisk are 15 feet thick at its base,

408
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:47,720
tapering to a thickness of only 18 inches at the top.

409
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:53,820
The tip of the monument includes a 100-ounce piece of cast aluminum,

410
00:25:54,480 --> 00:25:57,060
which at the time of the monument's completion in 1884

411
00:25:57,061 --> 00:25:59,060
was the largest in the Western Hemisphere.

412
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:06,240
At 555 feet, the monument was the world's tallest masonry structure.

413
00:26:10,420 --> 00:26:12,920
A similar challenge surrounded plans to build the Lincoln

414
00:26:12,921 --> 00:26:14,920
Memorial.

415
00:26:14,921 --> 00:26:17,220
And people were afraid that the soil couldn't support the

416
00:26:17,221 --> 00:26:18,720
Lincoln Memorial.

417
00:26:18,721 --> 00:26:22,860
And therefore, the Corps of Engineers stuck about 200

418
00:26:22,861 --> 00:26:25,660
concrete piers underneath the Lincoln Memorial to stabilize

419
00:26:25,661 --> 00:26:28,480
the monument and ensure that it didn't move or sink into the

420
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:30,180
soil.

421
00:26:30,181 --> 00:26:32,980
The memorial includes 36 columns,

422
00:26:32,981 --> 00:26:35,220
each 44 feet high.

423
00:26:35,221 --> 00:26:39,380
That's one column for each state in the Union in 1860, the year

424
00:26:39,381 --> 00:26:42,720
Lincoln was elected. Ironically, the experience of

425
00:26:42,721 --> 00:26:46,180
building monuments and creating a capital would have unexpected

426
00:26:46,181 --> 00:26:54,220
benefits during war. The Army Corps of Engineers'

427
00:26:54,221 --> 00:26:56,940
peacetime accomplishments paved the way for incredible

428
00:26:56,941 --> 00:27:04,740
proficiency on the battlefield. From the bridges and floating

429
00:27:04,741 --> 00:27:09,380
harbors of D-Day to the construction of the airstrips,

430
00:27:09,381 --> 00:27:14,980
bases, factories, hospitals, and housing for millions of

431
00:27:14,981 --> 00:27:18,460
soldiers in all of the nation's wars, the Corps has tackled an

432
00:27:18,461 --> 00:27:21,020
incredible array of daunting tasks, building the

433
00:27:21,021 --> 00:27:24,260
infrastructure of combat under extraordinary pressure.

434
00:27:24,261 --> 00:27:27,060
The construction of a road is much different in peacetime than

435
00:27:27,061 --> 00:27:30,900
it is in wartime, when you're under either small arms fire,

436
00:27:30,901 --> 00:27:34,500
or artillery fighter, or mortar fire.

437
00:27:34,501 --> 00:27:37,500
In the early days of World War II, the United States

438
00:27:37,501 --> 00:27:41,500
China defend itself from Japanese aggression.

439
00:27:41,501 --> 00:27:45,500
But when the Japanese seized Burma in May of 1942, China was

440
00:27:45,501 --> 00:27:50,500
in danger of being cut off from Allied ground support and supplies.

441
00:27:50,501 --> 00:27:53,500
Commanding General Joseph Stilwell had witnessed the capture

442
00:27:53,501 --> 00:27:58,500
of the previous land route, the Burma Road, by the Japanese.

443
00:27:58,501 --> 00:28:01,500
A new road into China had to be created.

444
00:28:01,501 --> 00:28:04,500
To keep supplies flowing into China, the United States decided

445
00:28:04,501 --> 00:28:07,540
The United States decided that they had to build a road from

446
00:28:07,640 --> 00:28:10,100
India, which was controlled by the British, the British

447
00:28:10,200 --> 00:28:15,240
colony, down into, through Burma and down into China.

448
00:28:15,340 --> 00:28:17,940
This road was called the Lido Road because it started out in

449
00:28:18,040 --> 00:28:22,160
Lido in Assam province in eastern India.

450
00:28:22,260 --> 00:28:24,460
The new road would have to pass through some of the most

451
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:27,560
treacherous terrain in the region.

452
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:31,500
The mountains in the Patke Range went from anywhere from

453
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:33,000
8,000 to 10,000 feet.

454
00:28:33,001 --> 00:28:40,040
There were two major valleys they had to cut through where there were leeches, malaria, and typhus mites to battle, as well as the rainfall.

455
00:28:40,740 --> 00:28:43,460
The rainfall in the valleys would be about 140 inches.

456
00:28:44,360 --> 00:28:50,220
Now to compare that 120 inches in the mountains, 140 inches in the valley for wet season,

457
00:28:50,221 --> 00:28:55,660
look at the eastern theater, the eastern coast of the United States, where you get an average rainfall of about 48 inches.

458
00:28:56,260 --> 00:28:58,920
Construction on the road began in December 1942.

459
00:28:58,921 --> 00:29:02,520
The road would be over 400 miles long.

460
00:29:02,620 --> 00:29:05,620
The most difficult part would be the first stretch from Lido

461
00:29:05,720 --> 00:29:09,560
down to Michina in Burma, about 275 miles

462
00:29:09,660 --> 00:29:14,600
through uncharted high mountains and deep valleys.

463
00:29:14,700 --> 00:29:17,340
Army Corps engineer Colonel Lewis Pick

464
00:29:17,440 --> 00:29:22,180
was brought in to tackle construction and bolster morale.

465
00:29:22,280 --> 00:29:25,120
Under his guidance, engineers worked around the clock

466
00:29:25,220 --> 00:29:27,480
to make up for lost time.

467
00:29:27,481 --> 00:29:31,080
What Pick did in his method of constructing the Lido Road

468
00:29:31,180 --> 00:29:34,120
was to bring in better equipment, heavier equipment,

469
00:29:34,220 --> 00:29:36,220
and also more engineer units.

470
00:29:36,320 --> 00:29:40,120
And then he would parcel out the engineer units

471
00:29:40,220 --> 00:29:42,520
into certain sections of the Lido Road

472
00:29:42,620 --> 00:29:45,600
and make them responsible for all that construction.

473
00:29:45,700 --> 00:29:48,200
In some of the most difficult mountain passages,

474
00:29:48,300 --> 00:29:50,940
engineers would climb 30 feet up to the outcroppings

475
00:29:51,040 --> 00:29:52,300
to place charges.

476
00:29:52,301 --> 00:29:57,340
charges. The resulting rubble from the

477
00:29:57,341 --> 00:30:02,240
explosion would then be cleared by hand or by bulldozer.

478
00:30:02,241 --> 00:30:05,040
Although the road engineers were subjected to dangerous working

479
00:30:05,041 --> 00:30:08,440
conditions and bad weather, nothing compared to devastating

480
00:30:08,441 --> 00:30:11,680
periodic attacks by the Japanese.

481
00:30:11,681 --> 00:30:14,780
Engineers had to be well versed in combat as well as road

482
00:30:14,781 --> 00:30:19,260
construction. When Japanese attacks increased,

483
00:30:19,261 --> 00:30:22,500
Stilwell devised a counteroffensive.

484
00:30:22,600 --> 00:30:27,700
In the spring of 1944, Stilwell brought in a provisional

485
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:29,440
regimental combat team.

486
00:30:29,540 --> 00:30:31,740
This was the famous Merrill's Marauders.

487
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:35,340
They were named after the Brigadier General Merrill,

488
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:37,120
who commanded this force.

489
00:30:37,220 --> 00:30:40,220
Their mission was to support the Chinese in attacking

490
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:43,220
to clear the path for the engineers to build the road.

491
00:30:43,320 --> 00:30:46,360
Merrill's force would attack into the rear of the Japanese

492
00:30:46,460 --> 00:30:48,720
defenses, set up a roadblock, and then

493
00:30:48,721 --> 00:30:53,820
And then the Chinese divisions would attack and hammer that

494
00:30:53,821 --> 00:30:58,520
defending Japanese force against that roadblock.

495
00:30:58,521 --> 00:31:02,960
Out of a force of 2,400 men, 93 Merrill's marauders

496
00:31:02,961 --> 00:31:05,960
lost their lives, and 293 were wounded.

497
00:31:09,680 --> 00:31:12,220
Overcoming incredible odds and adversity,

498
00:31:12,221 --> 00:31:14,440
the road from Lido to Kunming, China

499
00:31:14,441 --> 00:31:18,320
was completed in February of 1945.

500
00:31:18,321 --> 00:31:23,820
the toughest job ever given to US Army engineers in wartime.

501
00:31:23,821 --> 00:31:28,320
The engineers who built it referred to it as Pick's Pike.

502
00:31:28,321 --> 00:31:31,560
After the war, Lewis Pick would go on to become the Army Corps

503
00:31:31,561 --> 00:31:36,680
chief of engineers. In the 1950s, the Corps played

504
00:31:36,681 --> 00:31:41,580
an important role in another kind of war, the Cold War.

505
00:31:41,581 --> 00:31:44,880
The Corps became involved with missile site construction in

506
00:31:44,881 --> 00:31:49,120
construction in the early 1950s at the Atlantic Missile Test

507
00:31:49,121 --> 00:31:52,080
Range, which was on Cape Canaveral.

508
00:31:52,081 --> 00:31:55,120
In those early days, missile site construction was pretty

509
00:31:55,121 --> 00:31:56,920
primitive. I mean, it was a small launch

510
00:31:56,921 --> 00:31:58,920
site. The block houses were sometimes

511
00:31:58,921 --> 00:32:02,840
old tanks. Construction

512
00:32:02,841 --> 00:32:06,360
practices changed dramatically in the mid-'50s when the

513
00:32:06,361 --> 00:32:09,880
Intercontinental Ballistic Missile Program was implemented.

514
00:32:09,881 --> 00:32:12,380
The ICBM program was a crash program.

515
00:32:12,381 --> 00:32:16,280
There was a great urgency in building these silos as quickly

516
00:32:16,281 --> 00:32:17,620
as possible.

517
00:32:17,621 --> 00:32:22,720
Ultimately, the core built 1,200 ICBM silos.

518
00:32:22,721 --> 00:32:25,060
With its experience in missile site construction

519
00:32:25,061 --> 00:32:27,260
behind it, the Army Corps of Engineers

520
00:32:27,261 --> 00:32:30,260
was the logical choice to help lay a foundation for America's

521
00:32:30,261 --> 00:32:37,600
space program, beginning with the creation of NASA in 1958.

522
00:32:37,601 --> 00:32:39,980
NASA Administrator James Webb often

523
00:32:39,981 --> 00:32:44,480
space was paved with brick, concrete, and steel.

524
00:32:44,560 --> 00:32:48,380
And in that capacity, the Corps of Engineers had the expertise

525
00:32:48,460 --> 00:32:49,960
that NASA desperately needed.

526
00:32:53,100 --> 00:32:56,300
The Apollo program of the 1960s was one of the most

527
00:32:56,360 --> 00:33:00,460
ambitious ever devised, designed to carry man to the moon and

528
00:33:00,540 --> 00:33:02,260
return him safely.

529
00:33:04,380 --> 00:33:07,380
On-the-ground logistics for such a project were enormous.

530
00:33:07,381 --> 00:33:11,380
enormous. The Saturn booster,

531
00:33:11,381 --> 00:33:15,380
because it was so large, we needed a new infrastructure

532
00:33:15,381 --> 00:33:19,020
that would not only support the boosters, but support this huge

533
00:33:19,021 --> 00:33:23,020
workforce that would be needed to get man into space.

534
00:33:23,021 --> 00:33:26,940
The Corps began construction in Florida in 1963.

535
00:33:26,941 --> 00:33:29,740
The Kennedy Space Center, and more specifically,

536
00:33:29,741 --> 00:33:33,780
Launch of Complex 39, was orders of magnitude larger than

537
00:33:33,781 --> 00:33:37,380
anything that the Corps of Engineers had ever built.

538
00:33:37,381 --> 00:33:40,020
In order to transport the giant Saturn rocket boosters

539
00:33:40,120 --> 00:33:42,620
to Cape Canaveral, Florida from New Orleans, where they were

540
00:33:42,720 --> 00:33:46,180
tested and assembled, the Corps used the intercoastal waterway

541
00:33:46,280 --> 00:33:49,460
and excavated a mammoth 30-mile long channel

542
00:33:49,560 --> 00:33:51,100
through the Banana River.

543
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:53,760
The canal came down the intercoastal waterway,

544
00:33:53,860 --> 00:33:56,800
came across Merritt Island, and then up the Banana River.

545
00:33:56,900 --> 00:33:59,240
The fill that was taken from the canals

546
00:33:59,340 --> 00:34:01,640
was later spread over Merritt Island

547
00:34:01,740 --> 00:34:05,100
and in many places raised the height of the island by seven

548
00:34:05,200 --> 00:34:07,380
feet.

549
00:34:07,381 --> 00:34:11,380
Complex 39 was completed in the mid-'60s, and at the time was

550
00:34:11,381 --> 00:34:15,220
one of the largest structures on Earth.

551
00:34:15,221 --> 00:34:18,020
Today, the space shuttle is assembled in the very same

552
00:34:18,021 --> 00:34:21,620
vehicle assembly building, travels over the same crawler

553
00:34:21,621 --> 00:34:25,140
way, and thunders into space off of the same launch pads that

554
00:34:25,141 --> 00:34:27,140
hosted the Apollo program.

555
00:34:31,140 --> 00:34:34,540
The Corps has spent much of its 200-year history reshaping

556
00:34:34,541 --> 00:34:38,340
reshaping the United States. Yet its biggest challenge lies in

557
00:34:38,341 --> 00:34:42,340
restoring the natural environment it has worked so hard to control.

558
00:34:48,280 --> 00:34:51,080
Because of the incredible diversity of projects the Army

559
00:34:51,081 --> 00:34:53,400
Corps of Engineers has undertaken in its long and

560
00:34:53,401 --> 00:34:56,300
illustrious history, it continues to remain on the

561
00:34:56,301 --> 00:34:59,440
cutting edge of science and technology.

562
00:34:59,441 --> 00:35:02,400
Nothing reflects this more impressively than the Corps'

563
00:35:02,401 --> 00:35:05,240
Research and Development Center, a series of seven

564
00:35:05,340 --> 00:35:08,900
laboratories located across America.

565
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:13,040
The US Army Corps of Engineers has world-class laboratories,

566
00:35:13,140 --> 00:35:17,440
and we consider them the crown jewel of the Corps of Engineers.

567
00:35:17,540 --> 00:35:19,760
The Corps' sophisticated network of labs

568
00:35:19,860 --> 00:35:22,780
includes a coastal and hydraulics laboratory, which

569
00:35:22,880 --> 00:35:26,820
specializes in water engineering challenges.

570
00:35:26,920 --> 00:35:29,800
The construction of levees and dams evolved into the study of

571
00:35:29,801 --> 00:35:32,800
of soil and concrete, which ultimately evolved into the

572
00:35:32,801 --> 00:35:36,800
Geotechnical Instructures Laboratory.

573
00:35:36,801 --> 00:35:38,800
The Corps' state-of-the-art construction engineering lab

574
00:35:38,801 --> 00:35:42,800
specializes in military bases and customized airstrips that

575
00:35:42,801 --> 00:35:48,800
can be installed overnight in out-of-the-way places.

576
00:35:48,801 --> 00:35:51,800
Work in subzero climates led to the Corps' Cold Regions

577
00:35:51,801 --> 00:35:55,800
Research and Environmental Laboratory, formed to understand

578
00:35:55,801 --> 00:35:59,800
the effect freezing conditions have on armies and equipment.

579
00:35:59,801 --> 00:36:03,800
The Corps' advances in topographical engineering led to a

580
00:36:03,801 --> 00:36:07,800
lab which specializes in mapping, terrain analysis, and

581
00:36:07,801 --> 00:36:12,800
computer modeling, as well as an information technology lab,

582
00:36:12,801 --> 00:36:18,800
which specializes in high-speed computing.

583
00:36:18,801 --> 00:36:21,800
And at the environmental lab, work is being done on one of

584
00:36:21,801 --> 00:36:25,800
the Corps' most important mandates of the 21st century,

585
00:36:25,801 --> 00:36:29,400
restoring a compromised natural environment.

586
00:36:29,500 --> 00:36:31,700
To some, there is great irony here.

587
00:36:31,800 --> 00:36:35,240
The agency that built many of America's most monumental public

588
00:36:35,340 --> 00:36:38,640
works is now responsible for cleaning up the environmental

589
00:36:38,740 --> 00:36:41,840
mess it sometimes left along the way.

590
00:36:41,940 --> 00:36:45,560
Restoration is important to the core because practically all

591
00:36:45,660 --> 00:36:48,360
core projects do have a great effect on the environment in

592
00:36:48,460 --> 00:36:50,860
some way. Quite frequently, we alter the

593
00:36:50,960 --> 00:36:54,760
flow of water over an area, affect the hydrology.

594
00:36:54,761 --> 00:37:01,580
And it's important to try and go back and restore those areas in order to protect fish

595
00:37:01,581 --> 00:37:04,820
and wildlife resources and environment in general.

596
00:37:04,821 --> 00:37:08,620
It's no longer a process that only involves engineers.

597
00:37:08,621 --> 00:37:15,880
It involves botanists, it involves geologists, it involves people who are expert in silviculture.

598
00:37:15,881 --> 00:37:20,940
Because in order to design the right project nowadays, we have to understand nature, we

599
00:37:20,941 --> 00:37:24,940
We have to understand the natural forces, and we have to respect those forces.

600
00:37:24,941 --> 00:37:29,180
Case in point is the Everglades region of South Florida.

601
00:37:29,181 --> 00:37:33,940
In 1948, Congress ordered the Corps of Engineers to drain the Everglades.

602
00:37:33,941 --> 00:37:37,940
At the time, it was considered useless swampland, prone to flooding,

603
00:37:37,941 --> 00:37:41,940
to threaten the growing cities along Florida's coastline.

604
00:37:41,941 --> 00:37:46,940
Rivers such as the Kissimmee River were harnessed, and much of its water

605
00:37:46,941 --> 00:37:50,140
canals, levees, and other control structures away from the

606
00:37:50,141 --> 00:37:54,140
Everglades. This resulted in 1.7 billion gallons

607
00:37:54,141 --> 00:37:57,380
of fresh water being shifted out to sea every day.

608
00:37:57,381 --> 00:38:02,180
Today, nearly 70 different plant and animal species in the

609
00:38:02,181 --> 00:38:04,700
Everglades are fighting extinction.

610
00:38:04,701 --> 00:38:10,300
In the fall of 2000, Congress allocated $7.8 billion to the

611
00:38:10,301 --> 00:38:13,940
Army Corps of Engineers and the South Florida Water Management

612
00:38:13,941 --> 00:38:19,580
Water Management District to resuscitate the Everglades.

613
00:38:19,581 --> 00:38:22,540
This massive 20-year restoration process will include the

614
00:38:22,541 --> 00:38:25,080
breaching of dikes and barriers at the eastern end of the

615
00:38:25,081 --> 00:38:29,580
Everglades to allow the water to flow again, and work to restore

616
00:38:29,581 --> 00:38:33,600
the Kissimmee River is already in progress.

617
00:38:33,601 --> 00:38:36,500
The original core work many years ago resulted in a

618
00:38:36,501 --> 00:38:40,300
straightening of the river and virtually creation of a ditch.

619
00:38:40,301 --> 00:38:43,240
Today, that work is being reversed, and the core is

620
00:38:43,241 --> 00:38:45,880
is actually going in and restoring many of the old,

621
00:38:45,980 --> 00:38:48,280
cut-off side channels of the river

622
00:38:48,380 --> 00:38:51,880
and making it pretty much a meandering, crooked river

623
00:38:51,980 --> 00:38:53,480
the way it was many years ago.

624
00:38:56,580 --> 00:38:59,280
Across the continent, the Corps' own Bonneville Dam

625
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:03,000
is also the site of an ongoing restoration process,

626
00:39:03,100 --> 00:39:07,700
that of saving several species of salmon from extinction.

627
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:11,440
The Corps of Engineers first looked at the Columbia River

628
00:39:11,441 --> 00:39:18,240
system in the 1920s, it recognized that the dams would

629
00:39:18,340 --> 00:39:21,280
be a danger to the salmon.

630
00:39:21,380 --> 00:39:25,180
The Corps of Engineers built the very first fish ladders that

631
00:39:25,280 --> 00:39:28,900
efficiently moved salmon upriver.

632
00:39:29,000 --> 00:39:32,800
The problem was and remains getting them back down.

633
00:39:35,600 --> 00:39:38,540
Adult salmon were making it upstream to spawn,

634
00:39:38,640 --> 00:39:41,200
but younger fish were being lost in great numbers as they

635
00:39:41,201 --> 00:39:43,200
to negotiate the maze of river structures

636
00:39:43,201 --> 00:39:48,200
and head downstream. One obstacle for young fish

637
00:39:48,201 --> 00:39:50,200
was at the turbines of the power generator.

638
00:39:53,200 --> 00:39:57,200
Fish would pass through the turbines, sustaining major injury.

639
00:40:00,200 --> 00:40:03,200
A number of systems have been devised to help the juvenile

640
00:40:03,201 --> 00:40:08,200
fish population. Now what the Corps of Engineers

641
00:40:08,201 --> 00:40:11,700
What the Corps of Engineers have done is we have lowered and

642
00:40:11,701 --> 00:40:14,800
positioned some screens in the intake of the turbine.

643
00:40:14,801 --> 00:40:18,140
And what we're doing is we're redirecting a portion of this

644
00:40:18,141 --> 00:40:21,640
flow where the juvenile salmon are located up into this gate

645
00:40:21,641 --> 00:40:22,640
slot.

646
00:40:22,641 --> 00:40:24,960
And up into this gate slot, we have a means of collecting

647
00:40:24,961 --> 00:40:27,760
those fish and then transporting them downstream or passing them

648
00:40:27,761 --> 00:40:30,660
through the dam downstream.

649
00:40:30,661 --> 00:40:32,660
Once the fish have been collected,

650
00:40:32,661 --> 00:40:37,000
the Corps devised a way to reintroduce them into the river.

651
00:40:37,001 --> 00:40:42,000
the flow conditions correctly to look at the entering conditions into this area here.

652
00:40:42,001 --> 00:40:44,478
This is what we call a high flow outfall siding location.

653
00:40:44,479 --> 00:40:46,000
In other words, this is where we're

654
00:40:46,001 --> 00:40:51,000
going to release the fish once we capture them upstream and put them in downstream.

655
00:40:52,000 --> 00:40:54,753
There are two structures here for bypassing fish.

656
00:40:54,754 --> 00:40:57,000
One that is used in low water conditions

657
00:40:57,001 --> 00:41:00,389
and one that is used in high water conditions.

658
00:41:00,390 --> 00:41:04,000
And the total cost of this facility for bypassing

659
00:41:04,001 --> 00:41:06,240
the jeweled fish around the second powerhouse

660
00:41:06,340 --> 00:41:10,440
is about $82 million.

661
00:41:10,540 --> 00:41:13,600
At certain times of the year, even valuable hydropower

662
00:41:13,700 --> 00:41:17,140
is sacrificed to the cause of saving salmon.

663
00:41:17,240 --> 00:41:19,940
50,000 cubic feet of water per second,

664
00:41:20,040 --> 00:41:23,860
enough to generate 250,000 megawatts of power,

665
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:26,720
is allowed to run over the dam spillways in order

666
00:41:26,820 --> 00:41:28,360
to let the fish pass freely.

667
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:41,000
Today, the Corps employs a salmon research staff of 470,

668
00:41:41,001 --> 00:41:44,000
with a budget of $113.5 million.

669
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:52,000
For the Corps, the expression, built to last, meant a project

670
00:41:52,001 --> 00:41:55,000
was strong enough to withstand the test of time.

671
00:41:55,001 --> 00:41:58,000
But the work of environmental restoration has given built to

672
00:41:58,001 --> 00:42:03,000
last a new dimension, sustaining the natural environment.

673
00:42:03,001 --> 00:42:06,800
environment. Although Leopold,

674
00:42:06,801 --> 00:42:12,300
the great ecologist, talked about a round river, a system

675
00:42:12,301 --> 00:42:18,640
feeding into itself in harmony, in balance, sustainable, the

676
00:42:18,641 --> 00:42:22,860
core's challenge is to make that more than a metaphor, to build

677
00:42:22,861 --> 00:42:27,060
a system that can sustain itself. Nature evolves,

678
00:42:27,061 --> 00:42:31,460
science evolves, and so has the core, into an entity as dynamic

679
00:42:31,461 --> 00:42:35,700
ever-changing as the very forces it has worked so hard to harness.

680
00:42:35,800 --> 00:42:39,000
Therein lies the Corps' strength as well as its challenge,

681
00:42:39,100 --> 00:42:43,300
accomplishing the seemingly impossible in peace and war,

682
00:42:43,400 --> 00:42:47,500
on land and sea, in all terrains and climates.

683
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:49,620
With the dawn of the new millennium,

684
00:42:49,720 --> 00:42:52,820
the Corps' motto becomes more important and relevant than

685
00:42:52,920 --> 00:42:54,720
ever.

686
00:42:54,820 --> 00:42:56,720
Essayons, let us try.

